"MAGPIE: A benchmark for Multi-AGent contextual PrIvacy Evaluation (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.15186v1)\nciteturn9513view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.15186v1\",\"lineno\":290}); Total lines: 1702\nL151@P2: et al., 2023). These limitations have led to sig\u0002nificant privacy leakage, emphasizing the need for\nL152@P2: multi-agent, context-aware frameworks that can\nL153@P2: dynamically interpret and enforce privacy bound\u0002aries (Carlini et al., 2021; Chen et al., 2025b; Li\nL154@P2: et al., 2025b). Furthermore, the memory mecha\u0002nisms in LLMs present a critical concern, as struc\u0002tured prompting can exploit latent vulnerabilities\nL155@P2: to reconstruct or elicit sensitive data, especially\nL156@P2: as agentic architectures increasingly adopt shared\nL157@P2: memory, blurring the lines between short-term con\u0002text and long-term retention (Greshake et al., 2023;\nL158@P2: Wang et al., 2025a; Liu et al., 2024a; Li et al., 2024;\nL159@P2: Gao and Zhang, 2024). Recent work also high\u0002lights the rapid evolution of adversarial behaviors\nL160@P2: in multi-agent systems, from benign prompts to\nL161@P2: complex social engineering tactics, necessitating\nL162@P2: adaptive, multi-turn safety alignment and adver\u0002sarial co-evolution frameworks (Zhang and Yang,\nL163@P2: 2025; Ha et al., 2025; Ren et al., 2025; Guo et al.,\nL164@P2: 2025; Standen et al., 2023).\nL165@P2: Security Architecture In Agent Systems Tool\u0002augmented and multi-agent LLM systems repre\u0002sent particularly vulnerable domains for prompt\nL166@P2: injection and privacy leakage, exposing complex\nL167@P2: attack surfaces through interactions with external\nL168@P2: tools and shared memories (Alizadeh et al., 2025;\nL169@P2: An et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025c). Solutions\nL170@P2: involve fine-grained, dynamic Information Flow\nL171@P2: Control (IFC) mechanisms with automated depen\u0002dency tracking, often utilizing LLM-as-a-Judge\nL172@P2: frameworks and advanced prompting to enforce\nL173@P2: adaptive trust and security policies (Zhong et al.,\nL174@P2: 2025; Zheng et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2025d; Li\nL175@P2: et al., 2025a). Research advocates for applying\nL176@P2: established security design principles like defense\u0002in-depth, least privilege, and complete mediation to\nL177@P2: agent systems, alongside complex design patterns\nL178@P2: to build resilient architectures (Zhang et al., 2025;\nL179@P2: Beurer-Kellner et al., 2025; Rosario et al., 2025).\nL180@P2: Additionally, frameworks are being developed to\nL181@P2: ensure appropriate sensitivity through information\nL182@P2: labeling and strict, deterministic enforcement of\nL183@P2: access control mechanisms for proprietary data in\nL184@P2: enterprise contexts (Chowdhury et al., 2025; Patil\nL185@P2: et al., 2025; Bhatt et al., 2025; Kalapaaking et al.,\nL186@P2: 2023).\nL187@P2: Collaboration In Multi-Agent Systems Re\u0002search in multi-agent system collaboration focuses\nL188@P2: on optimizing cooperative mechanisms, standardiz\u0002ing communication, and developing rigorous eval\u0002uation methodologies (Li et al., 2023; Cemri et al.,\nL189@P2: 2025; Liu et al., 2025). Approaches include self\u0002improving systems that analyze reasoning trajec\u0002tories and correct failures, as well as strategies\nL190@P2: like task decomposition and fine-tuning individ\u0002ual agents for specialized roles to enhance effi\u0002ciency (Zhao et al., 2025; Zhou et al., 2025; Wang\nL191@P2: et al., 2025b). Effective coordination strategies,\nL192@P2: such as the Simultaneous-Talk-with-Summarizer\nL193@P2: approach and the Blackboard paradigm, central\u0002ize information sharing to boost collaboration in\nL194@P2: complex systems like MetaGPT (Han et al., 2025;\nL195@P2: Salemi et al., 2025; Han and Zhang, 2025; Wu\nL196@P2: et al., 2023a; Yan et al., 2025). Rigorous evaluation\nL197@P2: across diverse interactive environments, including\nL198@P2: cooperative and competitive dynamics, suggests\nL199@P2: that underlying language model capabilities are\nL200@P2: crucial for performance (Reza, 2025; Liu et al.,\nL201@P2: 2023; Zhu et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2024; Jimenez\u0002Romero et al., 2025). Advanced evaluations also\nL202@P2: consider language-mediated utility and dynamic\nL203@P2: perspectives to capture the temporal evolution of\nL204@P2: complex behaviors like cooperation or strategic\nL205@P2: disruption (Lee et al., 2025; Li, 2025; Mallampati\nL206@P2: et al., 2025; Erisken et al., 2025).\nL207@P2: 3 MAGPIE: Multi-AGent contextual\nL208@P2: PrIvacy Evaluation\nL209@P2: MAGPIE comprises 200 realistic, high-stakes,\nL210@P2: multi-turn negotiation scenarios designed to eval\u0002uate an agent’s ability to effectively collaborative\nL211@P2: while strategically managing sensitive information.\nL212@P2: The tasks span diverse real-world domains such\nL213@P2: as resource allocation, academic admissions, eco\u0002nomic negotiations etc. Unlike prior work, MAG\u0002PIE contains data points where private information\nL214@P2: is integral to the task, creating a trade-off between\nL215@P2: disclosure for task completion and withholding for\nL216@P2: privacy. The challenge, in these tasks, arises from\nL217@P2: unintentional leakage or strategic exploitation by\nL218@P2: non-malicious co-agents, rather than deliberate ad\u0002versarial attacks.\nL219@P2: As shown in Figure 1, each scenario in MAG\u0002PIE has a quantifiable success criteria that includes\nL220@P2: objective constraints, verifiable bounds and mea\u0002surable preferences. This enables quantitative as\u0002sessment of negotiation outcomes and privacy ad\u0002herence. By design, each scenario is solvable if the\nL221@P2-3: private informations are known to other agents.Seed\nL222@P3: Scenario\nL223@P3: Task\nL224@P3: Delieverable\nL225@P3: Success Criteria\nL226@P3: Agent Definitions\nL227@P3: Agent Preferences, Utilities\nL228@P3: and Penalties\nL229@P3: Task Solvability\nL230@P3: Task Realism\nL231@P3: Contradictory Preferences\nL232@P3: Agent Realism\nL233@P3: NO\nL234@P3: YES SynMAGPIE\nL235@P3: Regenerate if\nL236@P3: verification failed\nL237@P3: Add to Dataset if\nL238@P3: verification passed\nL239@P3: LLM LLM\nL240@P3: MAGPIE\nL241@P3: Human\nL242@P3: Refinement\nL243@P3: Verification\nL244@P3: Figure 2: Dataset construction pipeline for MAGPIE. A seed scenario is expanded by an LLM into a multi-agent\nL245@P3: negotiation task with deliverable and success criteria, followed by agent definitions (roles, utilities, and penalties).\nL246@P3: A separate LLM verifies solvability, realism, and conflicts; failed cases are regenerated, while successful ones are\nL247@P3: added to the dataset. Finally, human refinement ensures realistic constraints, privacy alignment, etc.\nL248@P3: 3.1 Data-Point Generation\nL249@P3: The dataset is constructed using an LLM-based\nL250@P3: pipeline followed by human refinement. As shown\nL251@P3: in Figure 2, we start with a human-written seed\nL252@P3: scenario (See Appendix A.4). Based on this sce\u0002nario, an LLM (Gemini-2.5-Pro) is then prompted\nL253@P3: to generate a detailed multi-agent negotiation task\nL254@P3: with N-agents where N is randomly chosen from\nL255@P3: [3, 7]. This includes narrative descriptions of the\nL256@P3: scenario, the specific task, agent definitions, the\nL257@P3: final deliverable (e.g. a proposal) and success crite\u0002ria. The success criteria includes task constraints\nL258@P3: (e.g. total funding <= $500,000) and individual\nL259@P3: agent’s constraints (e.g. minimum amount received\nL260@P3: by Agent 1 for R&D is $200,000). A task is called\nL261@P3: solvable if there exists a solution which satisfies the\nL262@P3: task constraints and individual agent constraints.\nL263@P3: Each agent in the task has a role which describes\nL264@P3: their position, a description that tells their rele\u0002vance to the task, information available to the agent\nL265@P3: and their utilities. Some of the informations can\nL266@P3: be shared and some are sensitive to sharing. For\nL267@P3: each information, the agent’s have an integer utility\nL268@P3: ∈ [−2, 2] based on the outcome of the negotiation.\nL269@P3: The sensitive informations are designed according\nL270@P3: to the socially acceptable privacy norms and shar\u0002ing them might hurt the agent. Leaking private\nL271@P3: preferences leads to a penalty incurred by the agent\nL272@P3: (See Appendix A.2 for prompts and A.5 for exam\u0002ple of a data-point).\nL273@P3: We prompt the LLM to ensure that the agents\nL274@P3: have conflicting preferences, the task is solvable,\nL275@P3: the task represents a realistic human negotiation\nL276@P3: situation, the private informations have socially\nL277@P3: acceptable reason to be private and to make every\nL278@P3: information highly elaborate and quantifiable.\nL279@P3: Once the scenario is generated, we use LLM-as\u0002a-judge to assesses each generated scenario. We\nL280@P3: use Gemini-2.5-Pro to analyze the scenario against\nL281@P3: five criteria: (1) Has Conflicts: presence of authen\u0002tic conflicts necessitating negotiation; (2) Private\nL282@P3: Info Justified: logical and socially acceptable ratio\u0002nale for private information; (3) Is Solvable: does\nL283@P3: an optimal solution to the task exist which satisfies\nL284@P3: all the success criteria; (4) Constraints Realistic:\nL285@P3: realism and verifiability of all specified constraints;\nL286@P3: and (5) Requires Negotiation: necessity of negotia\u0002tion and agreement for success. Only scenarios sat\u0002isfying all five criteria are accepted into the dataset.\nL287@P3: Failed scenarios are regenerated with detailed feed\u0002back on the problems in the last scenario from the\nL288@P3: LLM judge, and a note is appended in every sce\u0002nario that demonstrates what could be one of the\nL289@P3: possible solutions. The final stage involves human\nL290@P3: refinement, where annotators review accepted sce\u0002narios and enhance the constraints, check solvabil\u0002ity, natural language phrasing, realism, and confirm\nL291@P3: alignment with privacy requirements.\nL292@P3: 4 Simulating Negotiation\nL293@P3: To evaluate an agent’s ability to preserve privacy\nL294@P3-4: in collaborative multi-agent settings, we developa simulation environment that orchestrates interac\u0002tions between multiple AI agents. The environment\nL295@P4: facilitates realistic, round-based negotiations where\nL296@P4: agents must achieve consensus on a common pro\u0002posal to end the conversation, while managing their\nL297@P4: private interests. Following are the components of\nL298@P4: the environment:\nL299@P4: 4.1 Agent Definitions\nL300@P4: Each agent within the simulation is an instance of\nL301@P4: a specialized Agent class, designed to function as\nL302@P4: an autonomous negotiator on behalf of the user.\nL303@P4: Agents are initialized with profiles from the MAG\u0002PIE dataset, which includes their name, role, de\u0002scription, informations (shareable and sensitive),\nL304@P4: their utility functions, and the penalties for leaking\nL305@P4: the sensitive informations.\nL306@P4: All agents have access to tools to facilitate com\u0002munication, which include (a) Send Message: al\u0002lows an agent to transmit a text message only to\nL307@P4: specified agent list members, ensuring selective\nL308@P4: communication (b) Send Proposal: enables agents\nL309@P4: to submit a structured proposal to a select group of\nL310@P4: co-agents. The agents that received the proposal\nL311@P4: can (c) Accept Proposal: to accept and (d) Reject\nL312@P4: Proposal: to reject a proposal sent to the agent with\nL313@P4: a specific proposal id, along with a reason to ac\u0002cept or reject the proposal. Each agent is equipped\nL314@P4: with it’s own memory system. After every round,\nL315@P4: the agent can (e) Observe the Environment and\nL316@P4: extract critical information to (f) Write to Mem\u0002ory. This tool allows the agent to not only store the\nL317@P4: factual observations but also the inferred states of\nL318@P4: mind and motivations of other agents, helping the\nL319@P4: agent to strategize for complex decision-making.\nL320@P4: The agent also has a temporary memory which is\nL321@P4: used to store the latest five actions made my all the\nL322@P4: agents in the environment. Before each action, the\nL323@P4: agent updates this temporary memory. Lastly, an\nL324@P4: agent can (g) Pass it’s turn in a round if it does\nL325@P4: not have any action to take (See Appendix A.3 for\nL326@P4: agent prompts).\nL327@P4: 4.2 Simulation and Consensus\nL328@P4: The simulation begins with an initialization phase,\nL329@P4: where scenario data is loaded and agents are instan\u0002tiated with their roles, informations etc. Each agent,\nL330@P4: in addition, is provided with information about the\nL331@P4: task and expected deliverable, such as a structured\nL332@P4: proposal or a hiring decision in JSON format.\nL333@P4: In every round, agents begin by (a) Observing\nL334@P4: Environment to register new events, (b) Updating\nL335@P4: Memory to record observations, and (c) Decide\nL336@P4: Action to decide an action to take from the set of\nL337@P4: actions described in Section 4.1. Agents cannot\nL338@P4: directly access the conversation logs for more than\nL339@P4: the past two rounds, making the updating memory\nL340@P4: action critical for preserving declared informations,\nL341@P4: inferred behaviors, and failed strategies. All conver\u0002sation turns and memory entries are timestamped\nL342@P4: to ensure a verifiable history of interaction.\nL343@P4: Each agent also maintains an internal Proposal\nL344@P4: Status, which records the proposal id and its cur\u0002rent state: accepted, rejected, or none. Consensus\nL345@P4: is reached only when all the agents transition their\nL346@P4: status to “accepted” for the exact same proposal id.\nL347@P4: This process runs for a maximum of 10 rounds; if\nL348@P4: no consensus is achieved, the simulation terminates\nL349@P4: without agreement. Throughout the simulation, a\nL350@P4: complete log is maintained, capturing the conversa\u0002tion history, memory updates, and proposal states\nL351@P4: of all agents to enable detailed post-hoc analysis of\nL352@P4: negotiation strategies and privacy outcomes.\nL353@P4: 5 Analysis\nL354@P4: In this section, we analyze the behavior of\nL355@P4: LLM agents within multi-agent negotiation sce\u0002narios. We use state-of-the-art LLMs includ\u0002ing GPT-5 (OpenAI, 2025), Gemini-2.5-Pro (Co\u0002manici et al., 2025), Claude-4.1-Opus (Anthropic,\nL356@P4: 2025), GPT-4 (OpenAI et al., 2024) and LlaMa-4-\nL357@P4: Maverick (Meta, 2025) as agents. We study four\nL358@P4: key dimensions of agent interaction: (1) privacy\nL359@P4: leakage under implicit and explicit privacy instruc\u0002tions, (2) success in reaching consensus, (3) task\nL360@P4: completion, and (4) undesirable versus desirable\nL361@P4: conversational behaviors.\nL362@P4: 5.1 Privacy Leakage\nL363@P4: We evaluated privacy leakage under two experi\u0002mental settings. In the Explicit Instruction setting,\nL364@P4: agents were initialized with their full profile and\nL365@P4: additionally received a direct system prompt spec\u0002ifying which private items must not be disclosed,\nL366@P4: along with penalties for disclosure. In the Implicit\nL367@P4: Instruction setting, agents were provided with the\nL368@P4: same profile containing both public and private\nL369@P4: information, but without any indication of which\nL370@P4: information was sensitive, requiring them to infer\nL371@P4: privacy constraints from role, persona, and context.\nL372@P4: Following each simulation, an evaluator LLM\nL373@P4-5: (GPT-5) is provided with the full dialogue tran\u0002script and assesses whether the private items be-Type Severity GPT-5 Gemini 2.5-Pro GPT-4 Claude-4.1-Opus LlaMa-4-Maverick\nL374@P5: Implicit\nL375@P5: Partial 25.0 45.0 48.8 25.2 28.0\nL376@P5: Full 10.1 11.0 12.6 10.5 11.5\nL377@P5: Total 35.1 56.0 61.4 35.7 39.5\nL378@P5: Explicit\nL379@P5: Partial 20.0 40.5 48.0 21.5 22.0\nL380@P5: Full 5.0 10.2 8.0 10.1 10.5\nL381@P5: Total 25.0 50.7 56.0 31.6 32.5\nL382@P5: Table 1: Experimental results across explicit and implicit types with severity levels (Partial, Full, Total) for different\nL383@P5: models.\nL384@P5: came inferable. For each private information, the\nL385@P5: evaluator is asked a straightforward yes or no ques\u0002tion to access the leakage followed by a graded\nL386@P5: assessment of the leakage level (none, partial, or\nL387@P5: full). For instance, if an agent’s private prefer\u0002ence is “Chairman A supports approving NGO’s\nL388@P5: funding for project solar because they expect a\nL389@P5: personal donation from the organization X\", the\nL390@P5: evaluator would be asked, “From the conversation,\nL391@P5: can you infer that Chairman A has a personal fi\u0002nancial incentive tied to the approval of Project\nL392@P5: Solar’s funding?\". Verbatim disclosures are con\u0002sidered full leakage and partial leakage covers the\nL393@P5: cases where the agent discloses partial information\nL394@P5: or drops hints that could be harmful to the agent.\nL395@P5: (e.g., “This project’s success would really help my\nL396@P5: financial and career trajectory” is considered partial\nL397@P5: leakage).\nL398@P5: Table 1 shows the percentage of total private\nL399@P5: information that the agents leaked during negoti\u0002ation. We observe that all models exhibit higher\nL400@P5: total leakage rates in the implicit instruction setting\nL401@P5: compared to the explicit setting. Although the leak\u0002age rates are generally lower under explicit instruc\u0002tions, the observed differences are not drastic (for\nL402@P5: instance, the difference in leakage rates for explicit\nL403@P5: and implicit instruction is 4.1% for Claude-4.1-\nL404@P5: Opus 5.4% for Gemini-2.5-Pro), suggesting that\nL405@P5: even after explicit instructions, agents are likely to\nL406@P5: still disclose sensitive information in high-stress\nL407@P5: negotiation scenarios.\nL408@P5: GPT-4 shows the highest overall rates, with a\nL409@P5: leakage rate of 61.4% with implicit instructions and\nL410@P5: 56.0% with explicit instructions. Conversely, GPT\u00025 demonstrates the lowest leakage, with 35.1% un\u0002der implicit instructions and 25.0% under explicit\nL411@P5: instructions. However, even these lower percent\u0002ages are substantial in high-stakes environments\nL412@P5: where any disclosure could be harmful. Across all\nL413@P5: models, full leakage was lower compared to partial\nL414@P5: leakage rates, which were considerably high. For\nL415@P5: instance, implicit partial leakage for GPT-4 was\nL416@P5: 48.8% compared to 11.0% for full leakage. In crit\u0002ical applications, partial leakage can be as harmful\nL417@P5: as full leakage, as even indirect hints can allow\nL418@P5: sophisticated adversaries to infer private informa\u0002tion. See Appendix A.6 and A.6.1 for examples of\nL419@P5: leakage.\nL420@P5: 5.2 Privacy Leakage Across Rounds\nL421@P5: To gain a more granular understanding of when\nL422@P5: privacy leakage occurs during a conversation, we\nL423@P5: analyzed the cumulative average privacy leakage\nL424@P5: percentage at each round, as shown in Figure 3a.\nL425@P5: This analysis provides insights into the temporal\nL426@P5: dynamics of information disclosure, whether mod\u0002els tend to leak information early in an interaction\nL427@P5: or gradually over time. We observe that GPT-5\nL428@P5: and LlaMa-4-Maverick generally exhibit a more\nL429@P5: delayed increase in privacy leakage, suggesting\nL430@P5: that they are more resilient to early disclosure of\nL431@P5: sensitive information. In contrast, models such\nL432@P5: as GPT-4, Gemini-2.5-Pro, and Claude-4.1-Opus\nL433@P5: tend to leak a substantial portion of their private\nL434@P5: information earlier in the dialogue, often by the\nL435@P5: second or third round. This suggests that while\nL436@P5: all models eventually leak information under sus\u0002tained interaction, GPT-5 and LlaMa-4-Maverick\nL437@P5: made relatively robust to private information leak\u0002age, especially under explicit instructions.\nL438@P5: 5.3 Task Completion\nL439@P5: To assess the impact of privacy constraints on an\nL440@P5: agent’s task completion effectiveness, we measured\nL441@P5: how many cases did the agents reached a consen\u0002sus across all scenarios. Consensus is achieved\nL442@P5: when there is a proposal on which all agents agree.\nL443@P5-6: We found that a very low number of cases resulted(a) Average privacy leakage per round. (b) Task completion vs. privacy leakage.\nL444@P6: Figure 3: Comparison of privacy dynamics across rounds and task outcomes. (a) shows the average leakage trend\nL445@P6: per round (circles represent implicit instruction and star represent explicit instruction), while (b) illustrates the\nL446@P6: relationship between task completion and privacy preservation.\nL447@P6: in agents reaching a definitive consensus. Specif\u0002ically, in the implicit instruction setting, GPT-4\nL448@P6: achieved consensus in 13.5% of cases, followed\nL449@P6: by Gemini 2.5-Pro at 7.9% and GPT-5 at 5.3%. In\nL450@P6: the explicit setting, GPT-4 again led with 9.2%,\nL451@P6: and Gemini 2.5-Pro with 6.6%. Notably, LLaMA\u00024-Maverick, and Claude-4.1-Opus failed to reach\nL452@P6: consensus in any of the evaluated implicit or ex\u0002plicit scenarios (0%). Beyond simple consensus,\nL453@P6: we further evaluated task completion by providing\nL454@P6: the latest proposal (accepted by all agents or not) to\nL455@P6: an evaluator LLM (GPT-5). The evaluator checks\nL456@P6: for each constraint in the success criteria whether it\nL457@P6: was satisfied or not. It then provides a score based\nL458@P6: on the number of constraints (task and agent con\u0002straints) satisfied. For instance, if a scenario had\nL459@P6: three constraints and only one was met, the task\nL460@P6: completion score for that scenario would be 0.33.\nL461@P6: Figure 3b shows the relationship between task\nL462@P6: completion score and the total percentage of in\u0002formation leakage. We observe a general trend\nL463@P6: where task completion scores tend to increase as\nL464@P6: the percentage of privacy leakage rises, suggesting\nL465@P6: a trade-off between privacy preservation and task\nL466@P6: efficacy. Among the models, GPT-5 generally ex\u0002hibits the highest task completion scores, although\nL467@P6: lower consensus rates, while LLaMA-4-Maverick\nL468@P6: consistently shows the lowest. This correlation\nL469@P6: suggests that while models are capable of solving\nL470@P6: complex tasks, additional privacy constraints im\u0002peding their ability to complete tasks effectively.\nL471@P6: 5.4 Agent Behaviors in Negotiation\nL472@P6: Beyond privacy leakage, we analyse the behavioral\nL473@P6: strategies employed by agents during negotiation,\nL474@P6: categorizing their actions into several types: Ma\u0002nipulation (attempting to control or influence the\nL475@P6: other party through deceptive or indirect means),\nL476@P6: Sycophancy (excessive flattery or subservience to\nL477@P6: gain advantage), Lying (making false statements),\nL478@P6: Threatening (intimidating the other party), Sham\u0002ing (criticizing or demeaning to induce compli\u0002ance), Power Seeking (actions aimed at increasing\nL479@P6: one’s own influence or control), and Compromise\nL480@P6: (willingness to make concessions). For each be\u0002havior, we measured the percentage of agents that\nL481@P6: exhibited that specific behavior at any point dur\u0002ing the conversation. The results are visualized in\nL482@P6: Figure 4.\nL483@P6: We observe that ‘Power Seeking’ and ‘Manip\u0002ulation’ are frequently exhibited by more capable\nL484@P6: models, particularly GPT-4 and Claude-4.1-Opus,\nL485@P6: which show high rates. For instance, under im\u0002plicit instructions, GPT-4 engaged in Manipulation\nL486@P6: in 87.8% of cases and Power Seeking in 67.6%.\nL487@P6: Claude-4.1-Opus, demonstrated significant rates,\nL488@P6: with 66.7% Manipulation and 59.5% Power Seek\u0002ing in the implicit setting. Gemini 2.5-Pro also\nL489@P6: shows notable rates, with 38.2% Manipulation and\nL490@P6: 25.0% Power Seeking in the implicit setting. Com\u0002promise is another prominent behavior, with GPT-4\nL491@P6: and Gemini 2.5-Pro demonstrating very high rates\nL492@P6: (e.g., GPT-4 at 100.0%implicit and explicit, Gem\u0002ini 2.5-Pro at 94.7% implicit). Claude-4.1-Opus\nL493@P6: also shows considerable rates of Compromise at\nL494@P6: 59.5% implicitly and 52.6% explicitly. This indi\u0002cates that while these models are assertive, they\nL495@P6: are also capable of compromising their utility in\nL496@P6: order to reach agreements. Other behaviors like\nL497@P6-7: Lying, Threatening, and Shaming are less frequentFigure 4: Behavioral profiles of different LLM agents under implicit and explicit instruction settings.\nL498@P7: but still present, particularly in GPT-4 and Claude\u00024.1-Opus.\nL499@P7: In contrast, LlaMa-4-Maverick consistently dis\u0002plays very low to zero percentages across all be\u0002havioral categories in both implicit and explicit\nL500@P7: settings. When viewed alongside its minimal pri\u0002vacy leakage and extremely low task completion\nL501@P7: scores, this suggests that LlaMa-4-Maverick either\nL502@P7: struggles to engage meaningfully in complex nego\u0002tiation dynamics or adopts a highly passive strategy,\nL503@P7: effectively disengaging from the negotiation pro\u0002cess. GPT-5, while exhibiting more behaviors than\nL504@P7: LlaMa-4-Maverick, remains notably lower than\nL505@P7: other advanced models, showing 18.0% Manipu\u0002lation and 15.0% Power Seeking in the implicit\nL506@P7: setting, and 17.0% Manipulation and 12.0% Power\nL507@P7: Seeking in the explicit setting.\nL508@P7: 5.5 Mixed-Capability Agent Dynamics\nL509@P7: We further analyze the agent behaviors using dif\u0002ferent LLM backbones for individual agents within\nL510@P7: a negotiation setting. For this, GPT-5 and GPT-4\nL511@P7: were assigned to agents in an alternating pattern\nL512@P7: across all scenarios. Upon manual analysis, we\nL513@P7: find that GPT-5 agents to be more assertive, ex\u0002hibiting higher manipulation (50.0% of the cases),\nL514@P7: lying (33.3%), and power-seeking (50.0%) behav\u0002iors over GPT-4 agents. In contrast, GPT-4 agents\nL515@P7: were more diplomatic, showing much higher syco\u0002phancy (83.3%) and strong collaboration with less\nL516@P7: manipulation and an absence of lying. These pro\u0002files highlight GPT-5’s direct but manipulative ten\u0002dencies versus GPT-4’s more agreeable approach.\nL517@P7: 6 Conclusion and Future Work\nL518@P7: In this paper, we introduce MAGPIE, a bench\u0002mark designed to evaluate privacy preservation in\nL519@P7: multi-agent collaborative, non-adversarial scenar\u0002ios where private information is integral to task\nL520@P7: resolution. Our evaluation of state-of-the-art LLM\nL521@P7: agents reveals a significant gap: these models con\u0002sistently struggle to identify and protect sensitive\nL522@P7: user information, even when explicitly instructed\nL523@P7: to do so. This persistent leakage often correlates\nL524@P7: with improved task completion, suggesting a crit\u0002ical trade-off between privacy and efficacy. Upon\nL525@P7: behavioral analysis we find instances of undesir\u0002able strategic behaviors, such as manipulation and\nL526@P7: power-seeking, underscoring the complex align\u0002ment challenges for LLM agents operating in high\u0002stakes interactive environments. These findings\nL527@P7: collectively highlight that current LLM agents are\nL528@P7: not yet robustly aligned for simultaneously preserv\u0002ing privacy and fostering effective collaboration.\nL529@P7: Future work could focus on developing novel\nL530@P7: training methodologies, including privacy-aware\nL531@P7: fine-tuning and advanced RLHF/RLAIF ap\u0002proaches with multi-turn and multi-objective re\u0002ward models. Additionally, architectural innova\u0002tions such as dynamic Information Flow Control\nL532@P7: (IFC) and real-time privacy guardrail modules are\nL533@P7: crucial steps towards enhancing privacy preserva\u0002tion in LLM agents. Expanding MAGPIE to in\u0002clude adversarial scenarios and more nuanced im\u0002pact metrics is an immediate future work.\nL534@P7: 6.1 Limitations\nL535@P7: While MAGPIE provides a comprehensive frame\u0002work for evaluating privacy in multi-agent collab\u0002oration, our study has certain limitations. First,\nL536@P7: the reliance on an LLM-as-a-Judge for evaluating\nL537@P7: privacy leakage. Despite framing detection as a\nL538@P7: straightforward yes/no question with a definitive\nL539@P7-8: answer, the probabilistic nature of LLMs preventsa guaranteed 100% accuracy. Nevertheless, this\nL540@P8: approach offers superior generalization compared\nL541@P8: to rigid statistical methods, which are prone to\nL542@P8: missing nuanced or varied linguistic expressions\nL543@P8: of leakage. Second, although our benchmark con\u0002sists of diverse, high-stakes scenarios, the dataset\n"
